This directory holds benchmark output, and it is committed on purpose.

Every run writes four files sharing one run id:

  <run>.raw.json      every metric sample k6 took, one JSON object per
                      line, exactly as k6 emitted it
  <run>.summary.txt   the rendered summary, including its caveats
  <run>.summary.json  the same summary as data
  <run>.meta.json     the hardware stanza (see below)

Server logs (<run>.gateway.log and the two llmsim logs) land here too
while a run is being investigated, and are gitignored: they are a
debugging aid, not evidence.

THE HARDWARE STANZA

No result belongs in this directory without its .meta.json beside it.
That file records the CPU, logical core count, memory, operating
system, Go and k6 versions, the llmsim seed and time scale, which
profile the simulator was replaying and whether that profile was
calibrated or a built in guess, and every load knob the run used.

A latency number without that stanza is not a measurement anybody can
check. It is a claim. bench/run.sh writes the stanza before k6 starts,
so it exists even for runs that failed.

WHY THE RAW SAMPLES ARE COMMITTED AND NOT JUST THE SUMMARY

A summary is a set of choices about which statistics to show. Committing
the per sample JSON means a reader who distrusts those choices, or who
wants a percentile nobody printed, can recompute from the same data
rather than take the summary on faith. Every sample is tagged with its
run id, its scenario, and which arm of the comparison it came from.
